1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,160
Why does February
sometimes get an extra day?

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,880
Our calendar needs a small
adjustment to follow Earth's year.

3
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:13,220
Earth takes about 365.2422
days to go around the Sun.

4
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:16,660
That fraction matters when we
count whole days on a calendar.

5
00:00:17,817 --> 00:00:21,207
A regular calendar year has 365 days.

6
00:00:21,537 --> 00:00:24,587
So the calendar drifts by
almost a quarter day each year.

7
00:00:24,807 --> 00:00:26,337
The difference keeps building up.

8
00:00:27,317 --> 00:00:31,127
Adding an extra day every four
years fixes most of that drift.

9
00:00:31,462 --> 00:00:33,632
The extra day is February 29.

10
00:00:34,783 --> 00:00:36,653
But this adds a little too much time.

11
00:00:36,918 --> 00:00:41,428
The calendar needs an occasional exception
to keep that overcorrection small.

12
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:43,627
The Gregorian calendar,

13
00:00:44,057 --> 00:00:46,847
introduced in 1582, makes that adjustment.

14
00:00:47,192 --> 00:00:52,002
It skips leap days in century years,
unless the year divides evenly by 400.

15
00:00:53,217 --> 00:00:56,747
That rule explains why
1900 was not a leap year.

16
00:00:56,962 --> 00:00:59,532
But 2000 divided evenly by 400,

17
00:00:59,902 --> 00:01:01,292
so it kept its leap day.

18
00:01:02,317 --> 00:01:06,707
Leap years keep calendar dates close to
Earth's yearly cycle through the seasons.

